            <ArticleTitle>Outcome after a high number (4-10) of repeated caesarean sections.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>561-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate outcomes in caesarean sections repeated several times.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="POPULATION" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sixty-four women had had four or more (up to 10) caesarean sections.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The outcomes of these operations N= 149, study group) were compared with other caesarean sections.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A quarter of the women in the study group complained of low abdominal pains during the late pregnancy. A thin or fenestrated isthmic myometrial layer was observed in 55% of their operations. Abnormal placentation with an increased risk of major operative complications occurred more often in the study group. No differences in post-operative complications or perinatal outcome emerged between the groups.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Thus, no definitive upper limit for the number of caesarean sections per individual woman can be given.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
